    "excerpt": "It is rarely just your salary — lenders are solving for a specific ratio.",
    "readMinutes": 5,
    "relatedTool": "loan-eligibility-calculator",
    "content": [
      "Lenders generally cap loan eligibility using a fixed obligation-to-income ratio — commonly keeping total EMIs (including the new loan) below roughly 40-50% of monthly take-home income. This is why two people with identical salaries can be offered very different loan amounts if one already has other EMIs running.",
      "Tenure is the lever that moves eligibility the most for a fixed income. Stretching a loan from 15 to 20 years lowers the monthly EMI for the same principal, which can raise the maximum eligible loan amount considerably — at the cost of paying more total interest over the loan's life.",
      "Credit score acts less like a yes/no gate and more like a multiplier on the interest rate offered, which indirectly affects eligibility too: a lower rate means a lower EMI for the same loan amount, which frees up room under the obligation-to-income cap for a larger principal.",
      "## 1. The Fixed Obligation to Income Ratio (FOIR)",
      "When evaluating retail loan applications, banks do not simply look at your gross salary CTC; they calculate your **Fixed Obligation to Income Ratio (FOIR)**. FOIR represents the maximum percentage of your net monthly take-home salary that a bank will permit you to allocate toward all debt obligations combined.\n\nFor borrowers earning under ₹50,000/month, banks cap FOIR at 40% to 45%. For high earners above ₹1,50,000/month, lenders may permit a FOIR up to 55% or 60%.\n\n`Allowed New EMI = (Net Monthly Salary × FOIR %) - Existing Active EMIs`",
      "## 2. The Impact of CIBIL Credit Scores on Interest and Eligibility",
      "Your CIBIL credit score (ranging from 300 to 900) determines whether your loan is approved and directly dictates your sanctioned interest rate:\n\n- **750 to 900 (Excellent):** Qualifies for maximum sanctioned loan multipliers and lowest repo-linked interest rates.\n- **700 to 749 (Good):** Standard approval, but lenders may add a 0.25% to 0.50% risk spread to the interest rate.\n- **Below 650 (Poor):** High rejection probability; may require secondary co-applicants or collateral security.",
      "## 3. Worked Numerical Scenario: Income vs Existing Debt",
      "Consider a salaried applicant earning ₹90,000 net monthly salary with a ₹12,000 existing auto loan EMI:\n\n- **Assigned FOIR (50%):** Maximum allowed total debt = 50% of ₹90,000 = ₹45,000/month.\n- **Available EMI Headroom:** ₹45,000 - ₹12,000 existing car EMI = **₹33,000/month**.\n- At an 8.5% interest rate for 20 years, the per-lakh home loan EMI is approximately ₹868.\n- **Sanctioned Loan Eligibility:** (33,000 / 868) × ₹1,00,000 = **₹38.0 Lakhs**.\n\nIf the borrower pays off the ₹12,000 car loan before applying, their available EMI rises to ₹45,000, boosting their home loan eligibility from **₹38 Lakhs to ₹51.8 Lakhs**—an immediate ₹13.8 Lakh increase!",
      "## 4. Age, Tenure, and Co-Applicant Strategies",
      "Loan eligibility is constrained by the borrower's retirement age (typically age 60 for salaried employees and 65 for self-employed):\n\n- A 30-year-old applicant can comfortably qualify for maximum 30-year loan tenures, resulting in lower monthly EMIs and higher sanctioned loan amounts.\n- A 50-year-old applicant is limited to a 10-year tenure, sharply increasing the per-lakh EMI and reducing loan eligibility.\n- **Adding a Co-Applicant:** Adding a working spouse as co-borrower combines household incomes, increasing sanctioned eligibility by 60% to 90%.",
      "## 5. Frequently Asked Questions on Loan Eligibility",
      "### Does high credit card limit usage hurt loan eligibility?\nYes. Utilizing more than 30% of your total credit card limit indicates credit dependency and temporarily depresses your CIBIL score, reducing loan eligibility.\n\n### How does employment stability impact loan approval?\nLenders typically require a minimum of 2 years total work experience with at least 6 months at your current employer.\n\n### Can variable bonuses be included in loan eligibility calculations?\nMost commercial banks include only 50% of variable performance bonuses or average annual bonuses over the past two years when computing net monthly income.",
      "## 6. Strategic Takeaways",
      "Clear existing small personal and auto debts before applying for a major home loan. Pay down credit card balances to under 30% utilization to maximize your sanctioned borrowing capacity."
    ]
