      "The formula itself is simple — marks obtained divided by maximum marks, multiplied by 100 — but a common mistake is using the wrong maximum, especially in exams where some questions are optional or a paper has a maximum higher than what a student actually attempted.",
      "Another frequent error is averaging percentages across subjects with different maximum marks by simply averaging the percentages themselves rather than the underlying marks — this is usually mathematically fine only when every subject has the identical maximum; when maximums differ, weighting by actual marks (not just averaging percentages) gives the technically correct combined figure.",
      "Rounding errors compound this further: rounding each subject's percentage before averaging, versus averaging first and rounding once at the end, can produce a final result that differs by a fraction of a percent — usually immaterial, but occasionally enough to matter right at a cutoff boundary.",
      "## 1. The Real Cost of Conversion Errors in University Admissions",
      "Every year during university admission and job application seasons, thousands of candidate applications are disqualified during document verification due to incorrect marks-to-percentage conversions. With diverse educational boards, internal assessment rules, and grading frameworks in place, applying informal conversion shortcuts creates fatal compliance discrepancies.",
      "## 2. Mistake 1: Ignoring Additional Optional / 6th Subjects",
      "A frequent error among secondary school graduates is misinterpreting which subjects must be included in the aggregate percentage calculation:\n\n- **The 'Best of 5' Rule Misconception:** While boards like CBSE permit candidates to pass secondary examinations using a 6th vocational subject if they fail an elective, college admission cutoffs (especially Delhi University and state engineering bodies) mandate specific core subject combinations.\n- **The Regulatory Reality:** If an application portal requests **Overall Aggregate Marks**, you must include all subjects listed on your official marksheet—including compulsory language subjects—unless the admission guidelines explicitly state *'Aggregate of Best Four Academic Subjects'*.",
      "## 3. Mistake 2: Mixing Up Maximum Marks Across Theory and Practical",
