      "For anything with real consequences — a cross-board eligibility comparison for admissions or a scholarship — the safest approach is checking the specific board's published equivalence guidance rather than assuming a generic conversion rule applies, since institutions receiving the comparison often have their own equivalence tables too.",
      "## 1. The Multi-Board Educational Landscape",
      "In India and across several Commonwealth nations, secondary education is not governed by a single monolithic testing authority. Instead, students graduate from fundamentally distinct educational boards—predominantly the Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE), the Council for the Indian School Certificate Examinations (CISCE / ICSE / ISC), and over thirty distinct State Boards of Secondary Education (such as Maharashtra HSC, Tamil Nadu State Board, and UP Board).\n\nEach board operates under different pedagogical philosophies, curriculum frameworks, assessment syllabi, and grading standards, resulting in vastly different mark distributions for students of comparable intellectual capability.",
      "## 2. Comparing Pedagogical Philosophies: CBSE vs ICSE vs State Boards",
