      "## 3. The Moderation Policy and Marks Inflation Phenomenon",
      "Historically, boards practiced **moderation**—a statistical adjustment designed to equalize pass percentages across different question paper sets and compensate for unusually harsh paper checkers.\n\nHowever, between 2010 and 2017, competitive 'marks inflation' escalated among boards, with several authorities artificially inflating scores to help their state students secure competitive college admissions. Over 90,000 students nationwide began scoring above 95% in Class 12 board examinations, eroding the diagnostic utility of raw board marks and driving elite universities to adopt standardized entrance tests like CUET (Common University Entrance Test).",
      "## 4. Standardized Normalization Solutions for Admissions",
      "To resolve inter-board scoring disparities fairly in national engineering and medical selections, authorities rely on two main normalization strategies:\n\n1. **The Percentile Board Normalization Method:** Comparing students only within their own board's distribution. A student scoring 88% in a rigorous state board may rank in the 99th percentile of that board, granting them equal parity with a 95% CBSE scorer.\n2. **Universal Standardized Entrance Tests (CUET / JEE):** Administering a single common examination to all applicants regardless of schooling board, eliminating board-level evaluation biases entirely.",
      "## 5. Frequently Asked Questions on Board Grading Disparities",
