      "## 4. Worked Scenario: 4 Friends with Asymmetric Orders",
      "Consider a restaurant dinner with a ₹4,000 food subtotal, ₹400 tax (10%), and ₹600 tip (15% pre-tax tip). Grand Total = ₹5,000.\n\n- Friend A ordered: ₹1,600 (40% of food)\n- Friend B ordered: ₹1,200 (30% of food)\n- Friend C ordered: ₹800 (20% of food)\n- Friend D ordered: ₹400 (10% of food)\n\nUnder an equal split, each pays ₹1,250. Friend D (who ordered ₹400) is heavily overpaying by ₹750!\n\nUnder proportional allocation:\n- Total overhead (Tax + Tip) = ₹1,000 (which is 25% of the food subtotal).\n- Friend A pays: `1,600 + (1,600 × 0.25) = ₹2,000`\n- Friend B pays: `1,200 + (1,200 × 0.25) = ₹1,500`\n- Friend C pays: `800 + (800 × 0.25) = ₹1,000`\n- Friend D pays: `400 + (400 × 0.25) = ₹500`\n\nTotal collected = `2,000 + 1,500 + 1,000 + 500 = ₹5,000` exactly. Everyone pays their fair share.",
