      "ALL CAPS is readable for very short bursts — a button label, a warning, an acronym — but measurably slows reading speed for longer stretches of text, since capital letters remove the varied word-shape cues (ascenders, descenders) that readers rely on for fast recognition. It is a deliberate emphasis tool, not a default formatting choice.",
      "## 1. The Typographic and Computational Roles of Text Casing",
      "Text casing is far more than an aesthetic styling choice. In professional publishing, UX design, and computer programming, different casing conventions serve critical semantic and syntactic functions—signaling hierarchy, readability, or data object scope.",
      "## 2. Editorial Case Conventions in Human Writing",
      "Human-facing editorial typography utilizes four primary case styles:\n\n- **Sentence case:** Capitalizes only the first letter of the first word and proper nouns. Modern UX design frameworks (Apple Human Interface Guidelines, Google Material Design) recommend sentence case for UI labels and notifications because it improves reading speed and feels conversational.\n- **Title Case:** Capitalizes the first letter of principal words while keeping minor words lowercase (articles, prepositions under 4 letters, coordinating conjunctions). Title case is governed by style manuals:\n  - *Chicago Manual of Style:* Lowercases prepositions of any length (e.g. 'between', 'through').\n  - *AP Stylebook:* Capitalizes any word with 4 or more letters (e.g. 'With', 'From').\n- **UPPERCASE (ALL CAPS):** Reserves capitalization for short acronyms (NASA, UNESCO) and critical urgent warnings. Using full paragraphs of ALL CAPS reduces visual reading comprehension by 14-18% because uniform rectangular letter shapes eliminate ascending and descending ascenders.",
      "## 3. Programming Case Conventions in Software Engineering",
      "In computer code, spaces are invalid identifier delimiters. Programmers invented distinct casing styles to separate concatenated words:\n\n- **camelCase:** First letter lowercase, subsequent word beginnings capitalized (e.g. `userAccountBalance`). Standard in JavaScript, Java, and TypeScript for variables and functions.\n- **PascalCase (UpperCamelCase):** Every word capitalized (e.g. `UserProfileComponent`). Standard for class names, React components, and C# types.\n- **snake_case:** Words separated by underscores in lowercase (e.g. `calculate_tax_rate`). Universal in Python and SQL database column names.\n- **SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE:** Uppercase with underscores (e.g. `MAX_RETRY_COUNT`). Denotes global constants and environment variables.\n- **kebab-case (slug-case):** Words separated by hyphens (e.g. `how-age-is-calculated`). Standard in URL slugs, CSS class selectors, and REST APIs.",
      "## 4. Worked Typography Checklist for Digital Publishers",
      "When authoring web content:\n1. Page titles and `<h1>` tags: Use Title Case or Sentence Case consistently.\n2. Buttons and UI toggles: Use Sentence case ('Save changes' rather than 'Save Changes').\n3. URL paths: Always use lowercase kebab-case to prevent Linux server 404 case-sensitivity errors.\n4. Database table fields: Use snake_case to preserve SQL cross-engine portability.",
      "## 5. Frequently Asked Questions on Text Casing",
      "### Why are URLs case-sensitive on Linux but not Windows?\nWindows file systems (NTFS) are case-insensitive by default, whereas Unix/Linux web servers treat `/About` and `/about` as two completely separate file paths.\n\n### How do screen readers interpret ALL CAPS text?\nMany screen readers interpret capitalized words as acronyms, reading 'CONTACT US' out loud letter-by-letter as 'C-O-N-T-A-C-T U-S'.\n\n### What is 'Title Case' rule for hyphenated words?\nIn Chicago style, capitalize both elements of a hyphenated compound if each element is a major word (e.g. 'High-Quality Software').",
      "## 6. Strategic Takeaways",
      "Adopt sentence case for clean user interfaces and editorial clarity. In code and URL construction, strictly follow language-specific conventions like camelCase and kebab-case."
