      "Binary (base 2) is the actual language of digital hardware — every value in a computer's memory is ultimately stored as a sequence of on/off states, which binary represents directly. Humans rarely work with raw binary by hand, though, because long strings of 1s and 0s are hard to read and easy to miscount.",
      "Hexadecimal (base 16) exists as a human-friendly shorthand for binary, specifically because each hex digit maps exactly to four binary digits — converting between binary and hex is a simple grouping exercise with no arithmetic required, unlike converting binary to decimal, which needs real calculation. This is exactly why hex shows up constantly in color codes, memory addresses and byte values.",
      "Decimal (base 10) is simply what humans learned to count in, tied to having ten fingers, and has no particular technical advantage inside a computer — it exists in programming contexts purely for human readability, converted to and from binary at the boundary wherever a person needs to read or enter a value.",
      "## 1. The Physical Reality of Transistors: Base-2 (Binary)",
      "At the fundamental physical hardware level, modern silicon microprocessors are constructed from billions of MOSFET transistors. A transistor operates as an electronic switch with two stable states: non-conducting (low voltage, representing **0**) and conducting (high voltage, representing **1**).\n\nBecause electronic circuitry operates on bi-stable voltage states, digital computers are native **Base-2 (Binary)** machines. Every machine instruction, floating-point number, and memory address is physically stored and processed as sequences of binary bits (0s and 1s).",
      "## 2. The Human Ergonomics of Base-10 (Decimal)",
