      "Writing 64-bit binary memory addresses in raw ones and zeros requires strings of 64 characters (`010011010111...`), which are impossible for human engineers to read, debug, or transcribe without making errors. This led computer scientists to adopt **Hexadecimal (Base-16)**.\n\nHexadecimal uses digits 0 through 9 plus letters A through F (representing values 10 through 15):\n- The mathematical magic of Hexadecimal is that **16 is an exact power of two (`2^4 = 16`)**.\n- Therefore, exactly **four binary bits (a nibble)** map directly onto **one single hexadecimal character**!\n- An 8-bit computer byte (`2^8 = 256 values`) maps perfectly onto **exactly two hex digits** (`0x00 to 0xFF`).\n\n**Direct Conversion Demonstration:**\n- Binary: `1111 0011`\n- High nibble `1111` = 15 = `F`\n- Low nibble `0011` = 3 = `3`\n- Hex representation: **`0xF3`**\nNo arithmetic division or base math is needed—programmers can translate binary to hex visually in their heads instantly.",
      "## 4. Real-World Applications: Memory Addresses, CSS Colors, and Bytecode",
