      "## 5. Frequently Asked Questions on Numeral Systems",
      "### What is Octal (Base-8) and where is it still used?\nOctal uses digits 0-7, where each digit represents 3 binary bits (`2^3 = 8`). It survives in Unix file permission notation (e.g. `chmod 755`, where 7 = `rwx` = `111` in binary).\n\n### Why does hex notation prefix numbers with `0x`?\nThe `0x` prefix was introduced in the C programming language to signal to compilers that the subsequent characters represent base-16 literals rather than decimal variables.\n\n### How many colors can be represented in 24-bit TrueColor hex?\n24 bits equals 3 bytes (`0xFFFFFF`). `2^24 = 16,777,216` distinct color variations.",
